<div class="two-columns">
  <section class="card">
    <p class="eyebrow">LIFETIME</p>
    <h2>One client, one process</h2>
    <p>Puma uses workers=0 and 1–8 threads. <code>config.ru</code> constructs the client before serving; <code>at_exit</code> closes it after graceful shutdown. Do not close it after each request or preload an initialized client before worker forks.</p>
    <pre><code>at_exit { service.close }
# Native close stops refresh and flushes telemetry.</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section class="card">
    <p class="eyebrow">CURRENT DEFINITIONS</p>
    <h2>Polling and file snapshots</h2>
    <p>Native polling interval: <strong>
        <%= @service.configuration.refresh_interval %> seconds</strong>. Optional WebSocket and gRPC gems are not installed. The native file provider saves definitions; a restart can restore values while <code>ready</code> remains false if startup fetch fails.</p>
    <form action="/actions/refresh" method="post">
      <%= csrf_field %>
      <button class="secondary" type="submit">Request native refresh →</button>
    </form>
  </section>
</div>
<section class="card">
  <p class="eyebrow">USAGE + METRICS</p>
  <h2>Watch native batching locally</h2>
  <p>Native checks collect usage; the submission form records usage, measurement, counter and observation events. This sample injects a local transport through the public API. No telemetry is sent to Toggly in any mode, including connected mode. With no app key, the SDK does not start telemetry and batch counts stay zero. Counts describe flushed batches, not per-user or per-request totals.</p>
  <pre><code><%= h(JSON.pretty_generate(@service.telemetry.summary)) %></code></pre>
  <form action="/actions/flush" method="post">
    <%= csrf_field %>
    <button type="submit">Record view &amp; flush native telemetry →</button>
  </form>
  <p class="small">
    <code>variant:</code> is a caller-provided event label. It never performs allocation.</p>
</section>
<section class="card">
  <h2>Unknown feature behavior</h2>
  <p>
    <code>client.enabled?("unknown-flag", context: context)</code> → <%= state_badge(enabled?('unknown-flag')) %>
  </p>
  <p>Undefined defaults stay false even in development. <code>evaluate</code> returns the diagnostic reason <code>
      <%= h(@client.evaluate('unknown-flag', context: @context).reason) %>
    </code>; it does not supply the enabled check’s fallback semantics or automatic usage event.</p>
</section>
<%= partial('snapshot') %>
